A cell is a product of two closed intervals in the coordinates \((\lambda ,\bar{x})\) . Each interval has twelve root boxes \([\lambda_{0},\lambda_{\mathrm{hi}}]\times [\bar{x}_{i},\bar{x}_{i + 1}]\) ( \(0\leq i\leq 11\) ), where \((\bar{x}_{0},\ldots ,\bar{x}_{12}) = (2^{- 16},2^{- 12},2^{- 10},2^{- 8},2^{- 6},\frac{1}{8},\frac{2}{8},\ldots ,\frac{7}{8},1)\) , each covered by a binary subdivision (each piece split in \(\lambda\) , split in \(\bar{x}\) , or a leaf; a leaf may be bisected at most four more times).
